{"step_index":151,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:40:26Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-11T20:40:26+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-11T20:40:28+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/antigravity_handoff.py`\nTotal Lines: 348\nTotal Bytes: 15551\nShowing lines 100 to 200\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n100: \n101: \n102: # ── admissibility ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n103: #\n104: # An answer is compiled as the *returned file*, helper lemmas included — pasting\n105: # only the proof body back into the original, as the model campaign did, drops\n106: # every lemma a real proof needs and turns a correct proof into \"unresolved\".\n107: #\n108: # Compiling the returned file is only sound if nothing in it can change what is\n109: # being proved. The checks below close the routes that exist in Lean for that:\n110: # editing `Candidate` or anything it elaborates against; extending its term past\n111: # where it ended (a term does not stop at a newline); re-declaring `Candidate` or\n112: # the target inside a namespace; adding `variable` hypotheses the target silently\n113: # absorbs; redefining the tokens `Candidate` parses as; and bypassing the kernel.\n114: # The target's type is then re-checked by Lean itself, and the axiom audit rejects\n115: # `sorryAx` and anything non-standard.\n116: \n117: _CMD_START = (\"theorem\", \"lemma\", \"def\", \"abbrev\", \"example\", \"instance\", \"open\",\n118:               \"set_option\", \"attribute\", \"noncomputable\", \"private\", \"protected\",\n119:               \"@[\", \"#check\", \"#print\", \"structure\", \"inductive\", \"class\")\n120: \n121: _FORBIDDEN_CMD = re.compile(\n122:     r\"(?m)^\\s*(?:(?:private|protected|noncomputable|local|scoped)\\s+)*\"\n123:     r\"(namespace|section|variable|universe|notation\\w*|infixl?|infixr|prefix|postfix|\"\n124:     r\"macro\\w*|syntax\\w*|declare_syntax_cat|elab\\w*|initialize|builtin_initialize|\"\n125:     r\"run_cmd|run_elab|run_meta)\\b\")\n126: \n127: # Lean elaboration can run arbitrary code: `#eval IO.Process.run …`, `run_cmd`,\n128: # a user `elab`, an `@[init]` hook. Agents are only allowed to run the checker,\n129: # and the verifier compiles their files with full user privileges, so anything\n130: # that reaches IO or the metaprogramming API is refused *before* Lean sees the\n131: # file — that refusal is what makes \"only check.sh may run\" an actual boundary.\n132: _FORBIDDEN_TOKEN = re.compile(\n133:     r\"\\b(native_decide|ofReduceBool|trustCompiler|skipKernelTC|implemented_by|\"\n134:     r\"extern|axiom|unsafe\\w*|run_tac|run_cmd|run_elab|run_meta|addDecl\\w*|\"\n135:     r\"Lean|\\w*IO|System|Process|MetaM|CoreM|TermElabM|CommandElabM|TacticM|\"\n136:     r\"macro\\w*|elab\\w*|syntax\\w*|notation\\w*|initialize|builtin_initialize)\\b\"\n137:     r\"|#eval|#exit|\\bdebug\\.|@\\[\\s*(?:init|builtin_init|command_elab|term_elab|\"\n138:     r\"tactic|extern|implemented_by)\")\n139: \n140: _ALLOWED_IMPORTS = {\"Mathlib\", \"LeanProject.GraphInvariantsComputable\",\n141:                     \"LeanProject.GraphInvariants\"}\n142: \n143: \n144: def _norm(text: str) -> str:\n145:     return \"\\n\".join(line.rstrip() for line in text.replace(\"\\r\\n\", \"\\n\").split(\"\\n\"))\n146: \n147: \n148: def _strip_comments(src: str) -> str:\n149:     src = re.sub(r\"/-.*?-/\", \" \", src, flags=re.S)\n150:     return re.sub(r\"--[^\\n]*\", \" \", src)\n151: \n152: \n153: def _prefix(original: str):\n154:     \"\"\"Everything up to the first theorem: imports, options, `Candidate`.\"\"\"\n155:     i = original.find(\"abbrev Candidate\")\n156:     j = original.find(\"\\n\\ntheorem \", i) if i >= 0 else -1\n157:     return original[:j] if j >= 0 else None\n158: \n159: \n160: def structural_check(original: str, returned: str):\n161:     \"\"\"(ok, reason): may the returned file be compiled as an answer at all?\"\"\"\n162:     orig, ret = _norm(original), _norm(returned)\n163:     pre = _prefix(orig)\n164:     if pre is None:\n165:         return False, \"original challenge is malformed\"\n166:     if not ret.startswith(pre):\n167:         return False, \"statement changed: text above the first theorem differs\"\n168:     rest = ret[len(pre):]\n169:     if rest and not rest.startswith(\"\\n\"):\n170:         return False, \"statement changed: Candidate's last line was extended\"\n171:     code = _strip_comments(rest)\n172:     head = code.lstrip()\n173:     if head and not head.startswith(_CMD_START):\n174:         return False, \"statement changed: Candidate continues past its original end\"\n175:     m = _FORBIDDEN_CMD.search(code)\n176:     if m:\n177:         return False, f\"forbidden command `{m.group(1)}`\"\n178:     m = _FORBIDDEN_TOKEN.search(code)\n179:     if m:\n180:         return False, f\"forbidden construct `{m.group(0)}`\"\n181:     return True, \"\"\n182: \n183: \n184: def lint_text(text: str, original=None):\n185:     \"\"\"(ok, reason) for any Lean file an agent asks to elaborate.\n186: \n187:     A challenge file is held to `structural_check` against its original. Any\n188:     other file (scratch lemmas) gets the same code-execution screen plus an\n189:     import whitelist, since `import` is the other way to pull in code.\n190:     \"\"\"\n191:     if original is not None:\n192:         return structural_check(original, text)\n193:     code = _strip_comments(_norm(text))\n194:     for m in re.finditer(r\"(?m)^\\s*import\\s+(\\S+)\", code):\n195:         if m.group(1) not in _ALLOWED_IMPORTS:\n196:             return False, f\"import `{m.group(1)}` is not allowed\"\n197:     m = _FORBIDDEN_CMD.search(code)\n198:     if m:\n199:         return False, f\"forbidden command `{m.group(1)}`\"\n200:     m = _FORBIDDEN_TOKEN.search(code)\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
{"step_index":152,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T18:40:28Z","thinking":"**Exploring proof/refutation avenues**\n\nConsidering the implications of proving or refuting a specific statement. Examining how the `Candidate` is defined within the relevant context; this definition seems crucial.\nThe `Candidate` definition involves a universal quantifier over graph properties; this warrants close attention.\n\n\n**Questioning Candidate Statement Validity**\n\nConsidering the possibility of the `Candidate` statement being false; previous assumptions must be re-evaluated. Exploring whether there exists a graph where radius exceeds the total domination number, which would refute the `Candidate`. Initial explorations focused on specific graph types like random and grid graphs, which seem to support the inequality.\n\nExamining tree structures for examples where the total domination number can be smaller than the radius. Specifically, considering whether adding edges could decrease the total domination number while maintaining a relatively large radius.\n\n\n**Re-examining total domination proof**\n\nConsidering the implications of the total dominating set proof; each step needs careful scrutiny. Focusing on the case where a vertex, *x*, is connected to *b1*, and analyzing the distance from *u* to *x*. The proposed distance calculation, *u - a1 - b1 - x*, needs verification, especially when *x* is *b1*.\nThis highlights a possible flaw; perhaps the distance is smaller when x = b1? The total distance is less than or equal to 3.\n\n\n**Calculating vertex eccentricity bounds**\n\nConsidering the case where a vertex *x* is directly adjacent to *b1* but not *a1*; a path of length 3 from *u* to *x* can be constructed, implying a distance of at most 3. Analyzing subcase *B2* where *x* is connected to *b2* indicates a path of length 4, suggesting a distance bound of 4.\n\nThe total dominating set definition reveals that every vertex must have a neighbor within the set, implying that every vertex *x* belongs to either *V1* or *V2*, where distances are bounded by 3 and 4 respectively. Therefore, the eccentricity of *u* is at most 4.\n\n\n**Bounding radius by total domination number**\n\nThe prior reasoning demonstrates the radius is at most 4 when the total domination number is 4, radius is at most 2 when the total domination number is 2 or 3, and radius is at most 4 when the total domination number is 5. Now considering if the total domination number of 6 implies the radius can be greater than 6. A component tree of 4 components of size 2 is analyzed.\n\nThe worst-case scenario appears when all components are edges arranged in a path; the total domination is 8, and eccentricity is also less than or equal to 8. This leads to the conclusion that a radius is less than or equal to the total domination number for all cases.\n\n\n**Analyzing Path Structure and Distance**\n\nConsidering a path of edge components ($m$ = 4).  Determining the distance between a center component vertex and a vertex in the furthest component involves tracing a path through the connections between components. Re-calculating the edge counts between vertices in components, confirming the path length to a leaf vertex is 9. This distance seems to exceed the total domination number.\n\nRethinking the connection, and counting edges from one component to another while focusing on the worst-case scenario. This indicates the eccentricity can be 9, while total domination number is 8 in the example. This seems to provide a counterexample that radius can be greater. The prior test results for m=4 needs review.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python3 -c \"\nimport networkx as nx\n\nm = 4\nG = nx.Graph()\nfor i in range(m):\n    a = f'a_{i}'\n    b = f'b_{i}'\n    p = f'p_{i}'\n    q = f'q_{i}'\n    G.add_edge(a, b)\n    G.add_edge(a, p)\n    G.add_edge(b, q)\nfor i in range(m - 1):\n    u = f'u_{i}'\n    w = f'w_{i}'\n    G.add_edge(f'b_{i}', u)\n    G.add_edge(u, w)\n    G.add_edge(w, f'a_{i+1}')\n\nfor node in G.nodes():\n    ecc = nx.eccentricity(G, node)\n    if ecc == nx.radius(G):\n        print('Center:', node, 'ecc:', ecc)\nprint('Radius:', nx.radius(G))\n\"","Cwd":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_2a2555cc","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Finding centers for m=4","toolSummary":"Find center vertices for m=4"}}]}
